{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III of Tripoli was a powerful nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He served as regent for the Kingdom of Jerusalem while Baldwin IV suffered from leprosy. His political influence grew as he navigated the complex alliances between Christian and Muslim forces in the Levant. Raymond often clashed with other nobles who sought to increase their own power at his expense. He played a central role in the history of the Crusader states during a turbulent era.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born around 1140 as the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father was assassinated by the Hashashin sect in 1152 which left the young boy as the new count. He inherited a vulnerable county that faced constant threats from Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. Raymond spent his youth consolidating his power within Tripoli while seeking support from the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He eventually reached adulthood and began to assert his authority over neighboring territories. His marriage to Eschiva of Montfort further strengthened his position within the region.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe leprosy diagnosis of King Baldwin IV created a power vacuum that Raymond filled effectively. He became the primary regent for the young king and managed the affairs of the kingdom with skill. Raymond opposed the aggressive policies of Guy of Lusignan and his supporters. He sought peace with Saladin to protect the fragile Crusader states from total destruction. His diplomatic efforts often put him at odds with more militant factions within the Christian nobility. Raymond advocated for a cautious approach to warfare that prioritized survival over expansion. This stance made him many enemies among those who desired a more confrontational strategy against the Muslim armies.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Death*\nRaymond eventually reconciled with Guy of Lusignan before the disastrous Battle of Hattin took place in 1187. He fought alongside the other Crusader leaders against Saladin but managed to escape the encirclement that destroyed the main army. His survival allowed him to return to Tripoli and organize its defense against the victorious Muslim forces. Saladin besieged the city but failed to capture it due to Raymond's determined resistance. Raymond died shortly after the siege ended in 1187 under mysterious circumstances. Some sources suggest he died of illness while others imply he was poisoned by his enemies. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. The loss of his experienced leadership left a void that no other noble could fully fill during the subsequent crises.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0028659604}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.264979e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0096509345}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.289407}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.649173e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.21802433}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00011432824}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -1.7197609}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.013297}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.046235457}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.107986525}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.74376076}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.021534218}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.0005964627}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0015451919}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.01948629}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.0051846565}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -3.8147045e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.060668062}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.18285273}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.3561003}, {"token": " 